## Original User Request
**Description**: Research and define the AI-native execution philosophy for an MLE AI employee — how the role should be performed fundamentally differently when the'employee' is an AI agent rather than a human. This is NOT about managing the agent lifecycle, but about how the MLE role itself transforms. Investigate which human limitations (context-switching cost, cognitive load from juggling experiments, meeting overhead, deadline fatigue leading to technical shortcuts, career politics influencing technology choices, knowledge silos from imperfect memory) simply don't apply to an AI, and what that means for execution standards. Define the elevated quality bar: an AI MLE can produce mathematically optimal hyperparameter sweeps, maintain perfect experiment reproducibility, never cut corners on evaluation rigor, and hold entire codebase context simultaneously. Research how this transforms day-to-day activities — e.g., an AI MLE should never ship a model without exhaustive ablation studies because the time cost is negligible, should maintain perfect documentation as a natural byproduct, and should proactively detect drift rather than waiting for alerts.

**Todos**:
- Analyze which human cognitive limitations don't apply to an AI MLE: context-switching cost between experiments, meeting/communication overhead, deadline fatigue leading to shortcuts, imperfect memory of past experiments, career-motivated technology choices
- Define the elevated execution standard: no justification for incomplete ablation studies, every model ships with comprehensive model cards, perfect experiment reproducibility is the baseline not the aspiration, exhaustive evaluation across all relevant metrics before any deployment
- Research how continuous availability transforms the role: proactive drift detection (not reactive), continuous model health monitoring as a natural activity, instant response to production degradation, parallel experiment management at scale
- Define the philosophy around technical debt: an AI MLE should never accumulate ML-specific technical debt (unused features, stale models, undocumented pipelines) because the cost of doing it right is near-zero
- Investigate how perfect memory and context transforms collaboration: the AI MLE holds entire experiment history, every paper it has read, all past failure modes — implications for decision quality and knowledge sharing
- Research unique AI risks and compensating controls: overconfidence in automated decisions, need for human-in-the-loop checkpoints on novel architectures, maintaining interpretability of AI-generated model choices, avoiding runaway optimization without human alignment checks
</UserRequest>

