# MLE Operational Execution Model, Organizational Integration, and Performance Framework

## Comprehensive Research Report

---

## 1. Standard Operating Procedures for ML Model Development Lifecycle

### 1.1 MLOps Maturity Model & Lifecycle Framework

The ML model development lifecycle follows a three-loop model (established in Atlassian's MLOps frameworks):

**Inner Loop: Experimentation & Development**
- Data exploration and feature engineering
- Model development and architecture selection
- Model training with hyperparameter tuning
- Rapid iteration on small datasets
- Experiment tracking via MLFlow/Databricks

**Middle Loop: Integration & Validation**
- Feature productionization into feature stores (Tecton)
- Training pipeline automation (ML Studio workflows)
- Model evaluation with offline metrics
- Model registration in ML Registry
- Integration testing and validation

**Outer Loop: Production Deployment & Operations**
- Continuous model training and delivery
- Production serving via TEAMServe/SageMaker
- Monitoring, alerting, and feedback loops
- Automated retraining triggers

The target maturity is **Level 3: Automated Model Deployment** (Microsoft MLOps maturity model), characterized by:
- Automatic model releases via CI/CD pipeline
- Scoring scripts version controlled with tests
- Experiment results tracked
- Both training code and resulting models version controlled
- Full traceability from deployment back to original data

[Machine Learning Operations (MLOps) Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/VAPPDEV/pages/2166657676/Machine+Learning+Operations+MLOps+Framework)

### 1.2 Experiment Design & Code Review

**Experiment Workflow:**
1. **Use case registration** — Privacy approval + use case approval and provisioning in ML Studio
2. **Data access and preparation** — Data prep jobs in MLS with governance compliance
3. **Model training** — Via ML Studio workflows on Databricks/ML Fabric with experiment tracking
4. **Offline evaluation** — Eval provisioning and kickoff with baseline comparison
5. **Online A/B experimentation** — Model validation with launchpad (model start, response, and load test)
6. **Production deployment** — Through TEAMServe or SageMaker with canary analysis

**Code Review for ML:**
- MLEs produce PRs for model code, pipeline configurations, and feature definitions
- Reviews cover code quality (linting, modularity, reuse), test coverage, and ML-specific correctness
- Model training code and resulting artifacts are version controlled in Bitbucket
- ML Studio enforces strict data governance at the point of use

[MLOps user journey (part I) -- inner/middle loop](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120208a96defdad944d9aa72853f83f516b6a/pages/4727324664/MLOps+user+journey+part+I+--+inner+middle+loop)

### 1.3 Model Approval Gates & Staged Rollout

**Approval Gates:**
1. **Privacy/Use Case Review** — Required before data access; enforced by ML Studio governance layer
2. **Model Validation** — Offline evaluation metrics must meet baselines (precision, recall, AUC thresholds)
3. **Load Testing** — Model must pass launchpad testing (response time, throughput under load)
4. **Capacity Planning** — GPU/compute capacity verified before production deployment
5. **Pre-Prod Quality Gate** — Canary analysis of SLOs and SignalFx detectors after staging soak

**Canary Deployment Procedures:**
- Progressive rollout: 10% → 50% → 100% traffic with soak time at each stage
- Canary analysis monitors health signals (error rate, latency, CPU/memory)
- Automated rollback if anomaly scores breach thresholds (pass score: 70/100, marginal/fail: 50/100)
- Production deployments require manual judgment approval before canary stage
- Post-deployment verification via Pollinator checks and dashboard confirmation

[Canary Deployment Implementation Guide](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120203751105336c748498809ee74d33626f3/pages/6372694534/Canary+Deployment+Implementation+Guide)
[[DRAFT] Runbook: Safe Deployment Process with Spinnaker](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5672084560/DRAFT+Runbook+Safe+Deployment+Process+with+Spinnaker)

---

## 2. Guardrails and Escalation Policies

### 2.1 Model Performance Thresholds & Alerting

**Alert Severity Framework (exemplified by Abacus Model Inference SLOs):**

| Severity | Condition | Action |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| P3 (Warning) | Availability < 99.5% for 15 min | Slack notification |
| P2 (High) | Availability < 99% for 15 min | Page on-call |
| P1 (Critical) | Availability < 95% for 5 min | Page on-call + escalate |

**Alerting Rules Structure (from CoreML Long Term Plan):**
- Define alerting rules (e.g., latency > threshold, drift score > threshold)
- Define SOP (who's on-call to alert, what actions needed when pipeline failure)
- On-call roster establishment
- Manual triggers: MLE/MLSE initiate retraining based on monitored metrics
- Automated triggers: Pipelines launch retraining when thresholds are breached

[MLOPs@CoreML Long Term Plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202089f42605e1d342df8e8011fe153d216f/pages/5783505356/MLOPs+CoreML+Long+Term+Plan)
[Abacus Model Inference SLOs](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/teamce24763940c645e2880667b0495ad8e2/pages/6653970397/Abacus+Model+Inference+SLOs)

### 2.2 Bias/Fairness Detection Escalation

**Bias/Fairness Metrics** are tracked as part of the MLE technical metrics framework:
- Bias/Fairness metrics monitored alongside standard model metrics
- Model validation includes reviewing prediction distributions for skew vs. training data
- Responsible AI principles embedded in AI Platform north star goals
- ML Studio enforces strict data governance and privacy compliance

**Escalation Path:**
1. On-call engineer (immediate response)
2. DRI (Directly Responsible Individual) for the model
3. ML Platform team (infrastructure issues)
4. Responsible AI team (for policy violations)

### 2.3 Cost Budget Guardrails

**Cost Monitoring:**
- Model training/inference cost tracked as operational metric
- Resource utilization (CPU, GPU, memory, disk) monitored continuously
- Per-use-case quota enforcement on ML Fabric clusters
- Cost attribution back to user applications via Compass components
- Negative alerting on model usage (detecting unused models consuming resources)
- Platform utilization metrics per use case

**Cost Escalation:**
- Anomaly detection in cloud spending using ML algorithms
- Automated alerts when spending deviates from predicted norms
- Per-tenant rate limiting and resource isolation

[ML Fabric Infra Roadmap FY26](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6125263141/ML+Fabric+Infra+Roadmap+FY26)

### 2.4 Data Privacy Compliance Checkpoints

ML Studio enforces strict data governance:
- **Use case reviews** before data access
- **Data residency** compliance
- **Opt-out options** for customer data
- **Self-service data access** via HOT ticket for yellow/orange/red data
- **Retention policies** with automated purge
- **Workflow types** with different access levels: experimental (ad-hoc), released (production-governed)
- **Platform processes** that trigger high-severity alerts on purge failures

[ML Studio Introduction](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020e8e542e6fbb547dea43317e50413b60f/pages/4166496993/ML+Studio+Introduction)

---

## 3. KPIs and Success Metrics

### 3.1 Model Performance Metrics (from "Level Up your ML Craft")

**ML-Specific Metrics:**
1. **Experiment count & velocity** — Offline experimentations to improve model performance
2. **Offline performance** — Precision, Recall, AUC, F1, NDCG@K, MRR, MAP
3. **Online impact** — CTR lift, latency delta, engagement change
4. **Data coverage / quality metrics**
5. **Model reproducibility**
6. **Bias/Fairness metrics**
7. **Serving latency / model size / QPS metrics**

**Classification Metrics:** Accuracy, Recall, Precision, F1-Score, AUC-PR, AUC-ROC
**Regression Metrics:** MSE, RMSE, R-squared
**Ranking Metrics:** Precision@k, Recall@k, F1@k, NDCG@K, MRR, MAP

**Engineering-Specific Metrics:**
1. On-call / incidentownerships
2. Code quality (linting, modularity, reuse)
3. Test coverage (unit/integration)
4. PR velocity / PR count

[Level Up your ML Craft](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020b9c471b742f3414899397c898d2dd049/pages/5585955478/Level+Up+your+ML+Craft)

### 3.2 Inference Latency SLOs

**Standard SLO Targets (from production systems):**
- Availability: 99.5%
- P95Latency: < 3s (for model inference)
- P50/P90/P99 latency tracking per model
- Inference error rate per model, per error type

**Latency Profiling:**
- Offline latency profiling as pre-deployment signal (P50, P90, P99percentiles)
- Used to catch latency regressions before production deployment
- Training-serving latency comparison to detect serving skew

[Abacus Model Inference SLOs](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/teamce24763940c645e2880667b0495ad8e2/pages/6653970397/Abacus+Model+Inference+SLOs)

### 3.3 Training Cost Efficiency & Platform KRs

**Platform-Level KPIs:**
- Key search & chat workloads run30% more efficiently (performance and/or cost) on AI platform
- Number of scale-related incidents with TTR or mitigation > 3h (target: ≤1)
- ML Platform ready to support 200 tenants
- 10% reduction in TEAMServe model refresh turnaround time
- 10% reduction in dev agility measured by improvements in data, observability, and experimentation

**Observability Success Metrics:**
- 10% → 40% improvement in ML operational efficiency and pipeline reliability
- Number of model operational alerts registered
- % cost decreased from identifying workflow inefficiencies
- % e2e debugging time reduced from analyzing metrics
- TTD (Time to Detect) model issues improvement

[ML Observability Success Metrics](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5977515989/ML+Observability+Success+Metrics)

### 3.4 Deployment Metrics

- **E2E cycle time:** Time from data prep to model deployment
- **Deployment Frequency**
- **Code Coverage**
- **ML Lifecycle agility** — Measured from idea to production rollout

[ML Lifecycle agility](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120205e69934abf6c42eba7e4eb71c49179e8/pages/6799996615/ML+Lifecycle+agility)

