## 5. Common MLE Failure Modes and Mitigation Strategies

### 5.1 Training-Serving Skew

**Problem:** If the online feature store pipeline lags (e.g., Flink job delayed), the Online Store is stale but the Offline Store is "perfect." This makes training data "smarter" than production, causing Training-Serving Skew.

**Mitigations:**
- Monitor Freshness (System Time - Event Time)
- "Lag-offset" offline joins so the model trains on the same staleness it encounters in production
- Offline latency profiling as pre-deployment signal to catch latency regressions
- Point-in-Time (PIT) correctness with three-timestamp system (Event Time, System Time, Window Time)
- Online/offline data parity monitoring via ML Lens

**Real-World Example:** An incident with a 768-dimension model exhibited significantly higher prediction latency than a 128-dimension model, causing production degradation. Offline latency profiling could have caught this pre-deployment.

[Point-in-Time Correctness and Time Management in Feature Store for ML Models](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6453909241/Point-in-Time+Correctness+and+Time+Management+in+Feature+Store+for+ML+Models)

### 5.2 Data Drift

**Types of Drift:**
1. **Data drift (input distribution changes)** — Feature values in production shift vs. training
2. **Concept drift** — The mapping from features → outcome changes (e.g., infrastructure optimization changes the relationship)
3. **Product and ecosystem changes** — New features, configs, limits invalidate learned patterns
4. **Coverage gaps** — Model underperforms on new segments not in training data

**Mitigation Strategies:**
- Feature distribution drift monitoring via ML Lens
- Scheduled retraining (simple but risky if drift happens between cycles)
- Event-triggered retraining when accuracy drops or drift detected
- Dynamic thresholds based on historical score distributions
- Reference dataset strategy: Training Snapshot (detect training-serving skew) or Rolling Window (adapt to natural evolution)
- Automated alerts when drift scores breach thresholds

[Feature Drift Detection — Architecture Proposal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KYC/pages/6866709029/Feature+Drift+Detection+Architecture+Proposal)
[RFC: Model Re-training using Data Quality and ML Lens](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6013257858/RFC+Model+Re-training+using+Data+Quality+and+ML+Lens)

### 5.3 Silent Model Degradation

**Problem:** Models can degrade without triggering explicit errors — predictions still return but quality drops undetected.

**Mitigations:**
- Prediction distribution monitoring (detecting skew vs. training data)
- Online performance metrics: engagement, adoption, retention
- Custom real-time model evaluation and alerting
- Data freshness DQ prechecks to detect silently hanging pipelines
- Business metric correlation (e.g., correlate ML performance metrics with D1-D6, PPF)
- Freshness monitoring with fatal prechecks (pipeline fails fast with P2 alert if data is stale)

**Real-World Example (HOT-302133):** MLFS data staleness across multiple consuming services went undetected, causing incorrect metrics in dashboards. Mitigation: Added `fatalTableQualityPre` checks that fail pipelines immediately if data is stale.

[add freshness DQ precheck on LLM output table to detect LLM dag hanging](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/CSSDE-9191)

### 5.4 Feature Pipeline Failures

**Problem:** Feature pipelines can fail silently, producing stale or corrupt features that degrade model predictions.

**Mitigations:**
- Feature freshness monitoring via ML Lens
- Feature write job monitoring
- Feature quality monitoring and data quality checks
- Automated alerting on pipeline failures
- Data quality lineage tracking
- E2E hashing (SHA256) at each layer for data integrity validation
- Traffic slice monitoring at ingestion and serving layers

### 5.5 Resource Exhaustion

**Problem:** GPU/CPU exhaustion, SageMaker endpoint failures, auto-scaling misconfigurations.

**Real-World Example (PIR-300929):** SageMaker endpoint rebuilds occurred without notification, causing unpredictable failures. Unpinned dependencies in ML model packages allowed upstream changes to break production.

**Mitigations:**
- Auto-scaling re-tuning for SageMaker endpoints
- Dependency version pinning for ML model packages
- Automated rollback mechanisms for model deployments
- Safe-rollback wrapper scripts that preserve ASG floor
- Comprehensive runbooks and deployment documentation
- Per-use-case quota enforcement on ML Fabric clusters
- Resource utilization monitoring (CPU, GPU, memory, disk)

[PIR-300929 - HOT-301507 - Sev2 - Default L2: l2-serving-doc-v4failing from sagemaker](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/inckb/pages/6868629780/PIR-300929+-+HOT-301507+-+Sev2+-+Default+L2+l2-serving-doc-v4+failing+from+sagemaker)

### 5.6 Model Staleness

**Problem:** Models become stale when not retrained on fresh data, especially ranking models in dynamic environments.

**Mitigations:**
- Continuous model training and delivery (ML Studio Orchestrator)
- Metric-based retraining triggers using drift detection
- Model lifecycle management with expiry, grace period, and fallback metadata
- Automated deprecation/sunset policies with notification pipelines
- Nightly scanner jobs to detect upcoming model expirations

[Strategies for model lifecycle management](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6590159405/Strategies+for+model+lifecycle+management)

