## 6. Team Structure and Organizational Fit

### 6.1 MLE Role Levels and Scope

**Level Progression (based on Atlassian internal frameworks + industry mapping):**

| Level | Title | Experience | Scope | Key Differentiator |
|-------|-------|-----------|-------|-------------------|
| P40 | MLE | 3+ years | Individual ML features, model iterations within a team | Implements models, runs experiments, follows established patterns |
| P50 | Senior MLE | 4-8 years | End-to-end ML system (pipelines → serving) for a major product surface | Selects approaches, designs training pipelines, makes architecture trade-offs; tech leads2-3 projects |
| P60 | Principal MLE | 9-14+ years | ML strategy across multiple teams/product areas | Sets technical direction at org level, defines standards for production readiness |

**Key Scope Distinctions:**
- P40: Business problem quantification, model development and evaluations
- P50: System and model architecture design, experimentation, junior MLE guidance, cross-functional collaboration management
- P60: Tackle largest/most complex problems, determine plans of attack on large projects, solve complex architecture challenges, lead code reviews, partner across engineering teams

[Phase 1 Deep-Dive: Machine Learning Engineer (MLE)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TADT/pages/7026569052/Phase+1+Deep-Dive+Machine+Learning+Engineer+MLE)

### 6.2 MLE vs. MLSE Distinction

| Dimension | Machine Learning Engineer (MLE) | Machine Learning Systems Engineer (MLSE) |
|-----------|--------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| Focus | Model development and integration | Infrastructure and systems supporting models |
| Mix | ~30-50% ML, 50-70% engineering | ~70% engineering, 30% ML |
| Core Work | ML algorithms, experimentation, model evaluation | Data pipelines, deployment infra, scalability |
| Key Skills | PyTorch, TensorFlow, experiment design | Distributed systems, AWS, system architecture |

[MLSE Lunch and Learning](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~70121fa4d12f14797451589839ddf71b9d027/pages/4910851230/MLSE+Lunch+and+Learning)

### 6.3 Reporting Relationships & Team Placement

**Organizational Placement:**
- Core ML team sits within the **Data Technology Insights** group in the **Corporate Engineering Organization**
- Works cross-functionally across Growth, GTM, Product, Finance, and Sales organizations
- Team composition: ML engineers + system engineers (MLSEs)
- Cross-team roles include: Architect (2), Product Manager (1), Program Manager (0.5)

**Team Structure (from ML Platform):**
| Team | Mission | Typical Size |
|------|---------|-------------|
| ML Tools | Build/maintain core ML Platform (develop, train, evaluate, deploy, operate) | 1Manager + 8 Engineers (ideal: 2x MLE P5, 2x Sr. SWE P5, 2x SWE P4, 2x SWE P3) |
| MLOps | Deploy and operate models for teams without dedicated MLEs | Similar structure |
| MLaaS | Build systems for engineers to train/operate models without deep ML knowledge | Similar structure |

**Reporting Chain:**
- MLEs typically report to Engineering Managers (M3+)
- EM reports to Director/VP of Engineering within the relevant product or platform group
- Matrix dotted line to Product Manager for priority alignment

