# Machine Learning Engineer Role: Technical Foundations, Domain Expertise, and Operational Artifacts

## Research Comprehensive Report

---

## 1. Core MLE Technical Skills & Frameworks

### 1.1 Role Definition and Engineering Balance

Based on internal Atlassian role definitions, the MLE role is characterized by a **70% engineering / 30% machine learning** balance. The emphasis is on:

- **End-to-end ML lifecycle ownership**: Problem framing → data engineering → modeling → deployment → monitoring. This is a key differentiator — MLEs own the full cycle rather than handing off to backend teams for production deployment.
- **Production-quality software engineering**: Writing performant, scalable code for ML systems.
- **System design at scale**: Designing model architectures, feature pipelines, and serving infrastructure for millions of users.

[P35975- P50Senior Machine Learning System Engineer](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SER/pages/6172987623/P35975+-+P50+Senior+Machine+Learning+System+Engineer)

### 1.2 Core ML Frameworks (Required)

| Framework | Role in MLE Work | Growth Trend |
|-----------|-----------------|--------------|
| **PyTorch** | Primary deep learning framework;12.2x growth2021→2025 | Overtaking TensorFlow |
| **TensorFlow** | Legacy/alternative DL framework; 6.5x growth | Still common but declining mindshare |
| **JAX** | High-performance research/training | Emerging for specialized workloads |
| **Scikit-learn** | Classical ML (XGBoost, Random Forest, SVM) | Baseline/tabular data |
| **HuggingFace** | LLM/NLP model hub, transformers, fine-tuning | Primary for LLM workflows |
| **NeMo** | NVIDIA's toolkit for LLM training/fine-tuning | Used via Praxis ML integration |

[Questions for TechWolf Agent](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120207b67d459f263479392b6ccf6b9271069/pages/6671530707/Questions+for+TechWolf+Agent)

### 1.3 Data Engineering Stack

| Technology | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| **Apache Spark / PySpark** | Large-scale data processing and feature computation |
| **Databricks** | Primary ML workbench for experimentation, training, and notebook-based development |
| **SQL** | Data querying from Socrates (Atlassian's data lake) |
| **Delta Lake** | Data storage format within Databricks/vNext ecosystem |
| **dbt** | Data transformation and data product creation |
| **Airflow** | Workflow scheduling (being replaced by ML Studio orchestration) |
| **Kinesis** | Streaming data ingestion for real-time features |

### 1.4 Model Serving Technologies

| Technology | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| **AWS SageMaker** | Primary inference endpoints via Model Serving Platform (MSP) |
| **TEAMServe (Triton)** | In-house GPU-aware, multi-model serving platform (gRPC-based); replacing SageMaker for cost (~59% savings) |
| **AI Gateway** | Unified access to LLMs and foundation models (OpenAI, Google, Bedrock, Fireworks) |
| **Launchpad** | Quick model endpoint spinning for evaluation/testing |
| **Prediction Sidecar** | Locally-served model predictions in microservices |

[SageMaker Model Serving - Production Architecture v1](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/1883800569/SageMaker+Model+Serving+-+Production+Architecture+v1)
[Convo-AI Infrastructure Migration Tracker](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020541ef55388f64b6a9407024cfaf21362/pages/6791505931)

### 1.5 Programming Languages

- **Python** (primary): Production ML code, training scripts, inference logic
- **Java/Kotlin**: Backend integration, Spring Boot services (e.g., Tarot ML Registry)
- **SQL**: Data access and feature engineering
- **YAML**: Workflow definitions, service descriptors, configurations

